o I can  still give myself structure I can still  have goals and be accountable to someone  as a self-directed learner without  college and is a more challenging path  to take but I believe that there are  resources that support out there for  people who do want to take it say if  you're still teenager you're an early  child just google the term unschooling  and see what you find my favorite books  in the world of unschooling are from the  author john taylor gatto who was a  famous New York City public school  teacher and then he quit wrote an op-ed  to The Wall Street Journal and said I  don't want to teach kids I don't want to  teach anymore because I don't want to  hurt kids I defined unschooling as  full-time self-directed learning so  usually the term was applied in that k12  realm and legally unschoolers are the  same thing as home schoolers so they  fall under that umbrella they're subject  to specific state by state regulation  but when you unschool you are not taking  a formal curriculum and doing it at home  like a lot of people imagine  homeschooling to be it's about helping  your child identify and then pursue  their interests their passions their  curiosities and I believe that's a  philosophy that you can take all the way  up through the college years and if you  do it in a way that produces value for  other people you think like an  entrepreneur then it can self-directed  learning and unschooling can provide an  alternative to a college program
 

